-- title:
--   Lifting nilpotence of coordinates along a nilpotent thickening
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ and $S$ be commutative rings in a common universe, and let $\pi\colon R\to S$ be a surjective ring homomorphism whose kernel ideal $\ker\pi$ is a nilpotent element of the lattice of ideals of $R$, i.e. $(\ker\pi)^M=0$ for some $M$. Let $\sigma$ and $\iota$ be finite types, and let $\varphi\colon\iota\to R[[X_s:s\in\sigma]]$ be a finite family of formal power series in the variables indexed by $\sigma$ over $R$. Assume that there is an $N\in\mathbb{N}$ such that for every $s\in\sigma$ the power $X_s^N$, taken in $S[[X_s:s\in\sigma]]$, lies in the ideal generated by the set of coefficientwise reductions $\{\pi_*\varphi_j: j\in\iota\}$. The conclusion is that there exists an $N'\in\mathbb{N}$ such that for every $s\in\sigma$ the power $X_s^{N'}$, taken in $R[[X_s:s\in\sigma]]$, lies in the ideal generated by the range of $\varphi$. The exponent is not required to be the same on both sides: the proof produces $N'=NM$.
--
--   The statement is a descent assertion for the property that each coordinate is nilpotent modulo a finite family of power series, along a surjection with nilpotent kernel (a nilpotent thickening of the base). It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the formalisation, to transfer the existence of a kernel of bounded degree for a formal module from the reduction to the original base.
-- source:
